1
00:00:00,248 --> 00:00:04,748
In 1940, a brand-new bridge in
Washington State started to dance.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,866
Locals called it Galloping
Gertie, and people drove across

3
00:00:07,876 --> 00:00:09,426
just to feel it bounce.

4
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:12,350
Its main span was over half
a mile long, but its deck

5
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:14,132
was only eight feet deep.

6
00:00:14,142 --> 00:00:16,392
In the wind, it rippled like a ribbon.

7
00:00:16,527 --> 00:00:20,167
On November seventh, four months
after it opened, a steady wind

8
00:00:20,177 --> 00:00:23,002
of about forty miles
an hour began to blow.

9
00:00:23,103 --> 00:00:24,443
Then the motion changed.

10
00:00:24,453 --> 00:00:26,390
The bounce turned into a twist.

11
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:30,468
As the deck tilted, the wind caught
it at an angle and pushed it further.

12
00:00:30,478 --> 00:00:32,393
Every twist fed the next one.

13
00:00:32,403 --> 00:00:36,228
The wind was pumping energy in faster
than the bridge could lose it.

14
00:00:36,378 --> 00:00:38,527
This is real film from that morning.

15
00:00:38,628 --> 00:00:41,944
One man fled his car on the bridge,
with his daughter's dog, Tubby,

16
00:00:41,954 --> 00:00:43,204
still inside.

17
00:00:43,354 --> 00:00:46,804
Around eleven, the deck tore
loose and fell into the water.

18
00:00:47,062 --> 00:00:50,376
For decades, textbooks blamed
resonance, the wind matching

19
00:00:50,387 --> 00:00:51,686
the bridge's rhythm.

20
00:00:51,696 --> 00:00:52,536
It wasn't.

21
00:00:52,546 --> 00:00:55,822
The bridge made its own rhythm,
and the wind kept feeding it.

22
00:00:55,967 --> 00:00:58,882
That's why big bridges are
now tested in wind tunnels

23
00:00:58,892 --> 00:01:00,167
before they're built.

24
00:01:00,216 --> 00:01:02,041
So they never learn to dance.
